## X4  the named retry guard exists and is the same snapshot

$ grep -rn "ActiveSnapshotGuard::latest\|RegisteredSnapshotGuard::latest" src/
  src/am/ec_spire/custom_scan/planner.rs:542   ActiveSnapshotGuard::latest()
  src/am/ec_hnsw/build.rs:668                  ActiveSnapshotGuard::latest()
  src/am/ec_distann/custom_scan.rs:1271        RegisteredSnapshotGuard::latest()
  (plus ec_ivf/scan.rs:3922, ec_hnsw/scan.rs:1869, ec_diskann/scan_state.rs:412,
   physical_dml.rs:1499,1709,2040, generation_read.rs:458, traversal_replica.rs:1561)

$ sed -n '1,3p;18,23p;58,70p' src/storage/snapshot_guard.rs
  //! `RegisteredSnapshotGuard` owns register/unregister only, while
  //! `ActiveSnapshotGuard` also pushes/pops the backend active-snapshot stack.
  impl RegisteredSnapshotGuard {
      pub(crate) fn latest() -> Option<Self> {
          let snapshot = unsafe { pg_sys::RegisterSnapshot(pg_sys::GetLatestSnapshot()) };
  impl ActiveSnapshotGuard {
      pub(crate) fn latest() -> Option<Self> {
          let snapshot = unsafe { pg_sys::RegisterSnapshot(pg_sys::GetLatestSnapshot()) };
          if snapshot.is_null() { return None; }
          unsafe { pg_sys::PushActiveSnapshot(snapshot) };

Both constructors are `RegisterSnapshot(GetLatestSnapshot())`; `ActiveSnapshotGuard`
only adds the push/pop SPI needs. The plan's claim at request.md:155-157 —
"the active-SPI form of the same `GetLatestSnapshot` retry the current direct
fetch performs with `RegisteredSnapshotGuard::latest()`" — is accurate as
written. B1's "matching :1269-1285 attempt-for-attempt" is satisfiable by the
named mechanism.

## X5  B1 corruption arm is derivable locally, not only remotely

Same-transaction insertion is stated for both sidecar creation paths:
request.md:214-216 (sidecar heap/index creation shares the row/graph generation
transaction; Ready requires initial sidecar count == owned record count) and
request.md:221-225 (insert appends row-tier tuple, then its TID-keyed sidecar
tuple, in the same owner transaction; same-identity replacement appends a new
pair and never updates the old sidecar row). Neither tuple is ever deleted or
updated (request.md:229-231, retention derived from the row tier's append-only
rule), so the pair shares xmin and carries no xmax. "Visible row tier + no
visible sidecar" is therefore impossible except under corruption on the local
class for the same reason seq-02 accepted it on the remote class. The
candidate/control divergence on that arm is the ERROR B1 itself required.

