  :1213 if matches!(state.outputs.get(output_index), Some(RemotePending { .. }))
  :1217     materialize_pending_physical_window(state, output_index);
  :1327 fn materialize_pending_physical_window(state, output_index)
  :1331     let batch_size = super::options::materialization_batch_size();
  :1335     let (window_start, window_end) = pending_materialization_window(...)
  :1374     .materialize_remote_payload_pairs(&remote_pairs, &state.payload_attnums)
  :1733 CustomScanOutputRow::Frozen(hit.heap_tid)     (lazy build site)
  :1789 Some(CustomScanOutputRow::Frozen(hit.heap_tid)) (eager build site)

Mechanism named by the plan (request.md:82-89): batched at window
materialization in `materialize_pending_physical_window`, SPI
`unnest(row_tids, vec_ids) WITH ORDINALITY`, "at most one local SPI ... per
snapshot attempt and window, never SPI per row". B2 asked for exactly this
choice to be stated (batched-at-materialization vs per-row; SPI vs direct
index_beginscan). Both axes are now answered, and the answer is the non-per-row
branch, so B2's conditional "state why per-row planner cost does not confound
the primary endpoint" does not apply. The cost that remains — ~1-2 SPI
executions replacing ~4 direct `table_tuple_fetch_row_version` calls per
lazy-10 window — is carried inside the candidate arm of the §6 same-generation
A/B and is gated two-sided (>=5.0% AND >=0.50 ms on both pairs), so it cannot
silently inflate the primary endpoint.

Batching site is feasible against the current shape: `pending_materialization_window`
(:1422-1436) already returns `(output_index, min(window_start+batch_size,
proven))`, a window that spans local and remote outputs alike, so gathering
local pending rows from the same slice is a generalization of an existing loop,
not a new pass. Eager mode's single batch has a direct precedent at :1773-1775
(`materialize_remote_payloads(&hits, ...)` once over all hits).

